## 1. Kinematic arcade character controller WITHOUT a physics engine

The canonical reference is the official three.js example `games_fps.html` ("Octree threejs demo - basic collisions with static triangle mesh"). It uses **no physics engine** — gravity, integration, and collision are all hand-rolled against a static `Octree` built from a glTF scene.

- Source: https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/1bc25777ac82579be2137f1f2e3f7d649595405f/examples/games_fps.html

### 1a. Fixed-timestep accumulator / substep loop

`games_fps.html` does not use a free-running variable `dt`; it caps the frame delta and divides it into a fixed number of substeps so collisions can't be tunneled:

```js
const STEPS_PER_FRAME = 5;
// in animate():
const deltaTime = Math.min( 0.05, timer.getDelta() ) / STEPS_PER_FRAME;
// "we look for collisions in substeps to mitigate the risk of
//  an object traversing another too quickly for detection."
for ( let i = 0; i < STEPS_PER_FRAME; i ++ ) {
    controls( deltaTime );
    updatePlayer( deltaTime );
    updateSpheres( deltaTime );
    teleportPlayerIfOob();
}
renderer.render( scene, camera );
```

(All verbatim from `games_fps.html`.) The `Math.min(0.05, delta)` cap (50 ms) is the **spiral-of-death guard**: if the frame stalls, the sim refuses to step more than 50 ms of game time per frame, trading slow-motion for stability. This is exactly the pattern Fiedler prescribes ("clamp at a maximum # of steps per-frame and the simulation will appear to slow down under heavy load").

- Glenn Fiedler, **"Fix Your Timestep!"** — the canonical accumulator pattern with `accumulator += frameTime; while (accumulator >= dt) { integrate(state, t, dt); t += dt; accumulator -= dt; }` plus the `if (frameTime > 0.25) frameTime = 0.25;` clamp and the `alpha = accumulator / dt` render-interpolation factor: https://gafferongames.com/post/fix_your_timestep/
- Robert Nystrom, **Game Programming Patterns — "Game Loop"** ("Play catch up" section gives the identical `while (lag >= MS_PER_UPDATE) { update(); lag -= MS_PER_UPDATE; }` accumulator, and explicitly notes "you can safeguard this by having the inner update loop bail after a maximum number of iterations"): https://gameprogrammingpatterns.com/game-loop.html

The full classic accumulator (with render-state interpolation between previous and current physics state) is Fiedler's pattern; `games_fps.html` uses the simpler "cap + N substeps" variant, which is appropriate for an arcade toy game where you re-integrate every substep from the current velocity.

### 1b. Collider + collision resolution math (capsule vs triangle mesh)

The collider is a `Capsule` (a swept sphere: a line segment `start→end` plus a `radius`). The world is an `Octree` built once from the scene's triangles.

- `Capsule` addon (swept-sphere primitive; `intersectsBox` does the AABB broad-phase via `checkAABBAxis`): https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/1bc25777ac82579be2137f1f2e3f7d649595405f/examples/jsm/math/Capsule.js
- `Octree` addon (`fromGraphNode(scene)` builds the tree; `capsuleIntersect(capsule)` returns `{ normal, depth }` or `false`): https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/1bc25777ac82579be2137f1f2e3f7d649595405f/examples/jsm/math/Octree.js

The per-triangle test (`Octree.triangleCapsuleIntersect`) is the math worth copying: get the triangle plane, take signed distances from `capsule.start`/`capsule.end`; if both on the same far side, reject; otherwise lerp to the intersect point, test `triangle.containsPoint`; if that misses, fall back to closest-points between the capsule axis and each triangle edge (3 edges) and compare distance² to `radius²`.

```js
// From Octree.js (triangleCapsuleIntersect), abbreviated:
triangle.getPlane( _plane );
const d1 = _plane.distanceToPoint( capsule.start ) - capsule.radius;
const d2 = _plane.distanceToPoint( capsule.end ) - capsule.radius;
if ( ( d1 > 0 && d2 > 0 ) || ( d1 < -capsule.radius && d2 < -capsule.radius ) ) return false;
const delta = Math.abs( d1 / ( Math.abs( d1 ) + Math.abs( d2 ) ) );
const intersectPoint = _v1.copy( capsule.start ).lerp( capsule.end, delta );
if ( triangle.containsPoint( intersectPoint ) ) {
    return { normal: _plane.normal.clone(), point: intersectPoint.clone(), depth: Math.abs( Math.min( d1, d2 ) ) };
}
// else: closest-points of capsule axis vs the 3 triangle edges, depth = radius - dist
```

The aggregator `Octree.capsuleIntersect` then walks the candidate triangles the capsule overlaps, and for each hit **pushes the capsule out along the contact normal by `depth`** before testing the next triangle:

```js
capsuleIntersect( capsule ) {
    _capsule.copy( capsule );
    const triangles = []; let result, hit = false;
    this.getCapsuleTriangles( _capsule, triangles );
    for ( let i = 0; i < triangles.length; i ++ ) {
        if ( result = this.triangleCapsuleIntersect( _capsule, triangles[ i ] ) ) {
            hit = true;
            _capsule.translate( result.normal.multiplyScalar( result.depth ) ); // push out
        }
    }
    if ( hit ) {
        const collisionVector = _capsule.getCenter( _center ).sub( capsule.getCenter( _v1 ) );
        return { normal: collisionVector.normalize(), depth: collisionVector.length() };
    }
    return false;
}
```

This is a clean, copyable kinematic-vs-mesh resolution: **signed-distance plane test → point-in-triangle → edge-closest-points fallback**, accumulating penetration pushes.

### 1c. Ground detection + slope handling + integration

`games_fps.html` `playerCollisions()` / `updatePlayer()` is the complete recipe (verbatim, abbreviated):

```js
const GRAVITY = 30;
const playerCollider = new Capsule( new Vector3(0,0.35,0), new Vector3(0,1,0), 0.35 );

function playerCollisions() {
    const result = worldOctree.capsuleIntersect( playerCollider );
    playerOnFloor = false;
    if ( result ) {
        // slope threshold: walkable if the surface normal is within ~81° of up
        playerOnFloor = result.normal.y >= 0.15;
        if ( ! playerOnFloor ) {              // walls: kill velocity into the surface
            playerVelocity.addScaledVector( result.normal, - result.normal.dot( playerVelocity ) );
        }
        if ( result.depth >= 1e-10 ) {
            playerCollider.translate( result.normal.multiplyScalar( result.depth ) );
        }
    }
}

function updatePlayer( deltaTime ) {
    let damping = Math.exp( -4 * deltaTime ) - 1;   // frame-rate-independent velocity damping
    if ( ! playerOnFloor ) {
        playerVelocity.y -= GRAVITY * deltaTime;    // semi-implicit Euler gravity
        damping *= 0.1;                             // low air drag
    }
    playerVelocity.addScaledVector( playerVelocity, damping );
    const deltaPosition = playerVelocity.clone().multiplyScalar( deltaTime );
    playerCollider.translate( deltaPosition );      // integrate
    playerCollisions();                             // resolve
    camera.position.copy( playerCollider.end );
}
```

Key idioms/numbers (all from `games_fps.html`):

- **Ground = slope test on contact normal**: `result.normal.y >= 0.15` → walkable surface within `acos(0.15) ≈ 81°` of horizontal; vertical walls (`normal.y ≈ 0`) are not ground. Tune this single constant to set max walkable slope.
- **Wall slide**: when not on floor, project out the velocity component along the contact normal (`v -= n*(n·v)`), so the player slides along walls instead of sticking.
- **Penetration fix**: `playerCollider.translate(normal * depth)` after each contact.
- **Gravity** `30` units/s², **jump impulse** `playerVelocity.y = 15` (set, not added), **player radius** `0.35`, capsule from `y=0.35` to `y=1` (≈1.35-tall avatar).
- **Velocity damping**: `Math.exp(-4*dt) - 1` on the ground (damped), `*0.1` in air (near-frictionless) — exponential decay, frame-rate-independent (see §2).
- **Sphere-vs-triangle** path also exists: `Octree.sphereIntersect(sphere)` for projectiles/coins.

For a **sphere-vs-AABB** collider specifically (cheaper than a capsule for a rolling Wobble ball), the broad-phase is `Capsule.intersectsBox`/`checkAABBAxis` (https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/1bc25777ac82579be2137f1f2e3f7d649595405f/examples/jsm/math/Capsule.js — the `checkAABBAxis` helper), and `Octree.triangleSphereIntersect` (same Octree file) is the sphere-vs-triangle resolution you can lift directly: `sphere.intersectsPlane` → if `triangle.containsPoint(center)` return plane-normal & depth, else closest-point on each edge vs `radius²`.

`Octree.fromGraphNode(scene)` (same file) triangulates every `Mesh` (de-indexing via `toNonIndexed()`), bakes `matrixWorld` into the vertices, and builds the tree — call it **once** after loading the level; mutating level geometry requires rebuilding.

