## 4. Toy-like glossy Three.js visuals

### 4a. MeshPhysicalMaterial — clearcoat & sheen for plastic/vinyl (verified API + defaults)

`MeshPhysicalMaterial extends MeshStandardMaterial`. Verified source + JSDoc:

- https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/1bc25777ac82579be2137f1f2e3f7d649595405f/src/materials/MeshPhysicalMaterial.js

Relevant properties and their **defaults** (verbatim from the source JSDoc):

| Property | Default | Use for toy look |
|---|---|---|
| `clearcoat` | `0` | Set `1.0` for a glossy plastic/vinyl top coat ("car paints, carbon fiber, and wet surfaces" per JSDoc). |
| `clearcoatRoughness` | `0` | `0.0`–`0.15` for shiny; `0.3`+ for satin plastic. |
| `clearcoatMap` / `clearcoatRoughnessMap` / `clearcoatNormalMap` / `clearcoatNormalScale` | — | Per-pixel coat control. |
| `sheen` | `0` | Set `>0` (e.g. `0.5`–`1.0`) for a fabric/velvet rim; also reads as a soft toy sheen. |
| `sheenColor` | `0x000000` | Tint the sheen (e.g. white for plush). |
| `sheenRoughness` | `1` | Lower for a tighter sheen highlight. |
| `ior` | `1.5` | Drives `reflectivity` for non-metals (`reflectivity = clamp(2.5·(ior−1)/(ior+1), 0,1)`). |
| `iridescence` / `iridescenceIOR` / `iridescenceThicknessRange` | `0` / `1.3` / `[100,400]` | Optional pearlescent toy finish. |
| `specularIntensity` / `specularColor` | `1` / white | Non-metal specular control. |

JSDoc quote (verified): *"Clearcoat: Some materials — like car paints, carbon fiber, and wet surfaces — require a clear, reflective layer on top of another layer… Clearcoat approximates this effect, without the need for a separate transparent surface."* and *"Sheen: Can be used for representing cloth and fabric materials."* The same JSDoc also warns: *"For best results, always specify an environment map when using this material"* — which is why §4d matters.

Recommended toy-plastic starting point:

```js
new THREE.MeshPhysicalMaterial({
    color: 0xff5a3c, roughness: 0.35, metalness: 0.0,
    clearcoat: 1.0, clearcoatRoughness: 0.1,   // glossy coat
    sheen: 0.6, sheenColor: 0xffffff, sheenRoughness: 0.4, // soft toy rim
    envMapIntensity: 1.0,
});
```

### 4b. Rounded-box geometry (verified addon)

`RoundedBoxGeometry` is the ready-made toy-block shape:

- https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/1bc25777ac82579be2137f1f2e3f7d649595405f/examples/jsm/geometries/RoundedBoxGeometry.js
- Signature (verified JSDoc): `new RoundedBoxGeometry(width=1, height=1, depth=1, segments=2, radius=0.1)`. It `extends BoxGeometry`, makes `totalSegments = segments*2+1` (odd, so a flat plane connects the rounded corners), clamps `radius = Math.min(width/2, height/2, depth/2, radius)` so it can't blow past the box, and deforms a unit box's verts outward along their normalized corner direction to carve the rounded edges (see the `position/normal` loop in the constructor). UVs are generated per-face.
- Import: `import { RoundedBoxGeometry } from 'three/addons/geometries/RoundedBoxGeometry.js';`

For a perfectly round toy character body, `SphereGeometry` (r185: "Make pole vertices more robust" per release notes) or `CapsuleGeometry` are the built-ins.

### 4c. Soft shadows — `PCFSoftShadowMap` / `VSMShadowMap`, bias, mapSize (verified)

Shadow-map type constants are confirmed in the docs global index (https://threejs.org/docs/?q=MeshPhysicalMaterial lists `BasicShadowMap`, `PCFShadowMap`, `PCFSoftShadowMap`, `VSMShadowMap`). The official renderer config from `games_fps.html` (verbatim):

```js
renderer.shadowMap.enabled = true;
renderer.shadowMap.type = THREE.VSMShadowMap;          // soft, smooth shadows
renderer.toneMapping = THREE.ACESFilmicToneMapping;
// ...
const directionalLight = new THREE.DirectionalLight( 0xffffff, 2.5 );
directionalLight.castShadow = true;
directionalLight.shadow.camera.near = 0.01;
directionalLight.shadow.camera.far  = 500;
directionalLight.shadow.camera.right = 30; directionalLight.shadow.camera.left = -30;
directionalLight.shadow.camera.top   = 30; directionalLight.shadow.camera.bottom = -30;
directionalLight.shadow.mapSize.width  = 1024;
directionalLight.shadow.mapSize.height = 1024;
directionalLight.shadow.radius = 4;                     // PCF/VSM blur radius
directionalLight.shadow.bias   = - 0.00006;             // kills peter-panning/acne
```

- `PCFSoftShadowMap` is the classic soft choice; `VSMShadowMap` (what the example uses) gives smoother, blurrier shadows but can show light-bleeding on thin geometry — pick based on your level geometry.
- `shadow.bias` (negative, ~`-1e-4` to `-1e-6`) fixes shadow acne; `shadow.normalBias` (positive, ~`0.02`–`0.1`) is the companion knob for acne on sloped surfaces. `games_fps.html` shows `bias=-0.00006` and `radius=4` as a working starting point.
- The shadow **orthographic frustum** (`camera.left/right/top/bottom`) is set explicitly to ±30 — **tighten this to your level bounds** to maximize shadow-map texel density.

### 4d. ACESFilmic tone mapping + sRGB output + RoomEnvironment/PMREM (verified)

Color-management constants confirmed in the docs global index: `ACESFilmicToneMapping`, `AgXToneMapping`, `NeutralToneMapping`, `SRGBColorSpace`, `LinearSRGBColorSpace` (https://threejs.org/docs/?q=MeshPhysicalMaterial).

The canonical "good lighting for free" idiom is `RoomEnvironment` baked once via `PMREMGenerator` and assigned to `scene.environment`. Verified in two official examples:

- `RoomEnvironment` addon (a `Scene` with a `BackSide` room `Mesh`, six `InstancedMesh` boxes, six emissive area-light `Mesh`es via `createAreaLightMaterial`, and a `dispose()`): https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/1bc25777ac82579be2137f1f2e3f7d649595405f/examples/jsm/environments/RoomEnvironment.js
- `PMREMGenerator` source (verified API: `fromScene(scene, sigma=0, near=0.1, far=100, {size=256, position})` → returns a `WebGLRenderTarget`; you assign `.texture` to `scene.environment`. Internal: `HalfFloatType`, `LinearSRGBColorSpace`, default size `256`, uses **GGX VNDF importance sampling**, Heitz 2018): https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/1bc25777ac82579be2137f1f2e3f7d649595405f/src/extras/PMREMGenerator.js
- `webgl_instancing_dynamic.html` shows the **exact idiom** (verbatim):
  ```js
  const pmremGenerator = new THREE.PMREMGenerator( renderer );
  scene.environment = pmremGenerator.fromScene( new RoomEnvironment(), 0.04 ).texture; // sigma=0.04
  ```
  https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/1bc25777ac82579be2137f1f2e3f7d649595405f/examples/webgl_instancing_dynamic.html

The sigma `0.04` (a small blur in radians) is the soft-light default that appears across three.js examples (same value in `docs/scenes/material-browser.html`, verified at https://threejs.org/docs/scenes/material-browser.html: `scene.environment = pmremGenerator.fromScene( new RoomEnvironment(), 0.04 ).texture;`).

Renderer/color setup (combine with §4c shadows):

```js
const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer( { antialias: true } );
renderer.setPixelRatio( window.devicePixelRatio );     // clamp on mobile — see §6
renderer.setSize( window.innerWidth, window.innerHeight );
renderer.shadowMap.enabled = true;
renderer.shadowMap.type = THREE.PCFSoftShadowMap;       // or VSMShadowMap
renderer.toneMapping = THREE.ACESFilmicToneMapping;    // or NeutralToneMapping
renderer.toneMappingExposure = 1.0;                     // r185 default
// outputColorSpace defaults to SRGBColorSpace in r180+ (no set needed), and texture.colorSpace must be set to SRGBColorSpace for color (albedo) textures.
```

> `outputColorSpace` defaults to `SRGBColorSpace` (the correct value) in r180+, so you usually don't set it. For any **color/albedo** texture you load, set `texture.colorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace` (data/normal/roughness maps stay `NoColorSpace`). This is verified in `webgl_instancing_dynamic.html`: `texture.colorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace;`

### 4e. Cheap stylized outlines

three.js has a built-in outline addon: **`OutlineEffect`** (https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/tree/dev/examples/jsm/effects/OutlineEffect.js — listed in the docs Addons→Effects index at https://threejs.org/docs/?q=MeshPhysicalMaterial). For a cheap "inverted hull" toy outline without post-processing, the standard technique is a second `Mesh` with the same geometry, `material.side = BackSide`, `material.flatShading` off, scaled along normals (`scale = 1 + thickness/radius` for a sphere; or push verts along normals in a shader). `EdgesGeometry`/`LineSegments` give a crisp anime line but cost more for curved surfaces. The postprocessing path is `OutlinePass` (https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/tree/dev/examples/jsm/postprocessing/OutlinePass.js) or the TSL `outline()` node (`OutlineNode`, listed in the TSL docs index).

