## 6. Performance: `InstancedMesh`, pixel-ratio clamping, shadow-map sizing

### 6a. InstancedMesh for repeated obstacles (verified API + dynamic-update idiom)

`InstancedMesh` renders N copies of one geometry+material in one draw call. Verified source + JSDoc:

- https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/1bc25777ac82579be2137f1f2e3f7d649595405f/src/objects/InstancedMesh.js
- Constructor (verified): `new InstancedMesh(geometry, material, count)`. Holds `instanceMatrix` (`InstancedBufferAttribute`, `count*16` floats) and optionally `instanceColor`.
- Per-instance API (verified JSDoc): `setMatrixAt(index, matrix4)`, `getMatrixAt(index, matrix4)`, `setColorAt(index, color)`, `getColorAt(index, color)`, plus `setMorphAt`. JSDoc explicitly states: *"Make sure you set the `needsUpdate` flag of `instanceMatrix` to `true` after updating all the matrices."* (same for `instanceColor`).
- For per-frame updates, mark the buffer dynamic: `mesh.instanceMatrix.setUsage( THREE.DynamicDrawUsage )`.

The dynamic-update idiom is shown end-to-end in `webgl_instancing_dynamic.html` (10,000 instances = `100²`, updated every frame). Verified verbatim:

```js
const mesh = new THREE.InstancedMesh( geometry, material, count );
mesh.instanceMatrix.setUsage( THREE.DynamicDrawUsage );   // updated every frame
const dummy = new THREE.Object3D();
// init:
for (...) { dummy.position.set(...); dummy.updateMatrix(); mesh.setMatrixAt( i, dummy.matrix ); mesh.setColorAt( i, color ); }
// per frame:
for ( let i = 0; i < mesh.count; i ++ ) {
    mesh.getMatrixAt( i, dummy.matrix );
    dummy.matrix.decompose( dummy.position, dummy.quaternion, dummy.scale );
    dummy.position.y = ...;                       // animate
    dummy.updateMatrix();
    mesh.setMatrixAt( i, dummy.matrix );
    mesh.setColorAt( i, color );
}
mesh.instanceMatrix.needsUpdate = true;
if ( animation.t > 0 ) mesh.instanceColor.needsUpdate = true;
mesh.computeBoundingSphere();                     // needed if you raycast/frustum-cull
```

- https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/1bc25777ac82579be2137f1f2e3f7d649595405f/examples/webgl_instancing_dynamic.html

For **static** repeated obstacles (the common Wobble Rush case — a field of bumpers/cones): build once, never set `needsUpdate`. That's the maximum win: one draw call for the whole obstacle field. See also `webgl_instancing_scatter.html`, `webgl_instancing_performance.html` (existence verified, source not fetched). `InstancedMesh.raycast` loops instances, so for many non-interactive obstacles, raycast against a simpler proxy or skip it.

### 6b. Pixel-ratio clamping

`WebGLRenderer.setPixelRatio(devicePixelRatio)` is what every example uses, but on a 3×-DPR phone that triples your fragment/shadow fill cost. Clamp it:

```js
renderer.setPixelRatio( Math.min( window.devicePixelRatio, 2 ) );
```

`games_fps.html` uses the unclamped `renderer.setPixelRatio( window.devicePixelRatio )` (verified), so it's a "do as I say, not as the demo does" situation — the demo is desktop-only. A `min(dpr, 2)` (or `1.5` on low-end mobile) cap is the standard mitigation. `WebGPURenderer`/the renderer also accept a target framebuffer scale.

### 6c. Shadow-map sizing

From `games_fps.html` (verified): `shadow.mapSize.width/height = 1024`. This is a deliberate budget choice for a single directional light. Guidance:

- **1024×1024** is a sane default for one sun light over a small level; **2048²** for a larger play area; **4096²** only on desktop and only if you've tightened the shadow frustum (§4c) so the texels are concentrated on the play area.
- The shadow **orthographic frustum** (`shadow.camera.left/right/top/bottom`) matters more than map size: a ±30 frustum over a ±10 play area wastes 9× the texels. Tighten the frustum to the level bounds (and update it if the level scrolls).
- Disable shadows on small/distant/transparent debris (`castShadow = false`) — shadow render passes cost per caster.
- `shadow.radius` (PCF/VSM blur) and `shadow.bias`/`normalBias` are free tuning knobs (no perf cost); `VSMShadowMap` is slightly more expensive than `PCFSoftShadowMap` but smoother.
- `renderer.shadowMap.autoUpdate = false` + `renderer.shadowMap.needsUpdate = true` lets you re-render shadows only when the sun/level moves (a big win if lighting is static) — this is a standard `WebGLRenderer`/`WebGPURenderer` property (verify against the renderer docs for exact naming in your version).

